  "image_name": "3e42aff0abfbe01e052eb1df4abb1563.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a bilayer ionomer coating designed to improve CO₂ electroreduction on a copper catalyst. A cation-exchange ionomer and an anion-exchange ionomer are combined to form an ionomer bilayer film on top of a sputtered copper layer supported by a porous PTFE membrane, with CO₂-saturated electrolyte above. The central idea is that this layered interface modifies the local reaction environment in complementary ways.\n\nThe upper zoom-in suggests that the cation-exchange region promotes Donnan exclusion, which appears to restrict ion transport in a way that traps OH⁻ near the interface. This is presented as increasing ethanol selectivity by controlling the local ionic environment around the ionomer backbone and charged side chains. The lower zoom-in shows CO₂-rich pathways extending through the film toward the electrocatalyst surface, indicating improved CO₂ access to the catalyst. That increased local CO₂ availability is linked to a higher reaction rate.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys a synergistic mechanism: one layer helps manage hydroxide and selectivity, while the other helps deliver CO₂ efficiently to the copper surface, together enhancing catalytic performance.",
